<section xml:id="sec-mod2-assessment">
  <title>Assessment</title>
  <p>Estimated time: 40 minutes.</p>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->

    <p>
      This assessment has three parts. Parts 1 and 2 ask you to write
      complete mathematical proofs, submitted for feedback. Part 3 is
      a short reflection. Submit all three parts together as a single
      document (PDF or typed file) through the course management system.
    </p>
    <p>
      <alert>Write-up standard.</alert> All proofs must be written in
      complete mathematical prose. Use the style modeled in the Worked
      Examples: include scratch work (clearly labeled) before the formal
      proof, announce your strategy, write in complete grammatical
      sentences, and justify every step. Work consisting only of symbolic
      manipulations without explanation will not receive full credit.
    </p>
    <p>
      <alert>A note on Part 2.</alert> All students complete all three
      parts. Part 2 offers two options. Option A asks you to show that
      scaling a set by a positive constant scales its supremum, by
      checking both conditions of the definition. Option B asks you to
      derive the Archimedean Property from the Completeness Axiom by
      contradiction, and is the more demanding. Choose the option that
      will stretch you.
    </p>
    <p>
      <alert>Academic integrity.</alert> Your proofs must be your own
      work. You may refer to your notes, the textbooks, and the worked
      examples from this module. You may not collaborate with other
      students on the written proofs or use AI writing tools to draft
      your arguments.
    </p>
  <paragraphs xml:id="par-mod2-as-part1">
    <title>Part 1: Core Proof (Required)</title>
    <p>
      Estimated time: 20 minutes.
    </p>
    <p>
      Consider the set
      <md>S = \{x \in \R : x^2 + x \lt 6\}.</md>
    </p>
  </paragraphs>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod2-as-1a">
    <title>Analyzing the Set</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Factor the inequality <m>x^2 + x - 6 \lt 0</m> and determine
        exactly which real numbers belong to <m>S</m>. (That is: what
        familiar interval is <m>S</m>?)
      </p>
      <p>
        Show your algebra clearly. This scratch work does not need to
        be in formal proof style; it is the analysis step that precedes
        the proof.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <hint>
      <p>
        Find the two roots of <m>x^2 + x - 6</m> to factor it. Then
        use the fact that a product of two real numbers is negative
        exactly when the factors have opposite signs.
      </p>
    </hint>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod2-as-1b">
    <title>Proving the Supremum</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Using your work in the previous problem, prove that
        <m>\sup S = 2</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Your proof must have the following structure:
        <ol marker="(i)">
          <li>
            <em>Scratch work (clearly labeled).</em> Verify that 2
            is an upper bound. Then: given <m>\varepsilon \gt 0</m>,
            find an explicit element of <m>S</m> greater than
            <m>2 - \varepsilon</m> (for small enough <m>\varepsilon</m>).
            Show the algebra.
          </li>
          <li>
            <em>Formal proof.</em> Using your scratch work, write
            a complete proof that <m>\sup S = 2</m> by verifying
            both conditions of the <m>\varepsilon</m>-characterization.
            The proof should be written in complete mathematical prose.
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <hint>
      <p>
        For condition (ii), you need a point of <m>S</m> strictly
        between <m>2 - \varepsilon</m> and <m>2</m>; a point defined by
        a simple formula in <m>\varepsilon</m> is easiest to check.
        To show your point lies in <m>S</m>, use the factored form from
        the previous problem and find the sign of each factor. Your
        point may fail to lie in <m>S</m> when <m>\varepsilon</m> is
        large, so say how your proof handles large <m>\varepsilon</m>.
      </p>
    </hint>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod2-as-1c">
    <title>The Infimum</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        State (without proof) the value of <m>\inf S</m>. Then write
        one sentence explaining why, for this particular set, you did
        not need the Completeness Axiom to know that <m>\sup S</m> and
        <m>\inf S</m> exist in <m>\R</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->

  <paragraphs xml:id="par-mod2-as-part2">
    <title>Part 2: Proof Choice (Required; Choose One)</title>
    <p>
      Estimated time: 15 minutes.
    </p>
    <p>
      Choose <em>one</em> of the following two proof problems.
      Both ask for a complete proof; they differ in difficulty and
      the techniques required. Label your submission clearly with
      <q>Option A</q> or <q>Option B.</q>
    </p>
  </paragraphs>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod2-as-2a">
    <title>Option A (Standard): Scaling a Supremum</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>S</m> be a nonempty subset of <m>\R</m> with
        <m>s = \sup S</m>. Let <m>c \gt 0</m> be a positive real number,
        and define <m>cS = \{cx : x \in S\}</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Prove that <m>\sup(cS) = cs</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Your proof must verify both conditions in the definition of
        supremum for the set <m>cS</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <hint>
      <p>
        Check the two conditions of the definition one at a time,
        starting each from what you know about <m>S</m> and <m>s</m>.
        For the second condition, take an arbitrary upper bound of
        <m>cS</m> and produce from it an upper bound of <m>S</m>.
        Say where you use <m>c \gt 0</m>.
      </p>
    </hint>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod2-as-2b">
    <title>Option B (More Challenging): The Archimedean Property</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Assuming the Completeness Axiom, prove the Archimedean Property:
        for every <m>x \in \R</m>, there exists <m>n \in \N</m> such
        that <m>n \gt x</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Use proof by contradiction. Your proof should clearly identify:
        what set the Completeness Axiom is applied to, what supremum
        is obtained, and what contradiction is derived from the
        <m>\varepsilon</m>-characterization.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <hint>
      <p>
        Start by writing down what the negation of the conclusion
        says about <m>x</m> and <m>\N</m>; that tells you which set
        the Completeness Axiom applies to. Then choose a value of
        <m>\varepsilon</m> suited to the spacing of the natural
        numbers, and look for an element of that set that is too
        large.
      </p>
    </hint>
  </exercise>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->

  <paragraphs xml:id="par-mod2-as-part3">
    <title>Part 3: Reflection</title>
    <p>
      Estimated time: 5 minutes.
    </p>
    <p>
      Write 3<ndash/>5 sentences for each question. These are graded
      for thoughtfulness, not for mathematical correctness.
    </p>
  </paragraphs>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod2-as-r1">
    <title>The Role of Completeness</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        In Part 1 you identified <m>\sup S</m> and <m>\inf S</m> directly,
        without the Completeness Axiom. Where in this module is the
        Completeness Axiom genuinely needed? Name one result or
        exercise whose proof uses it, and say exactly where in that
        proof it enters.
      </p>
      <p>
        Then consider: could you have written your Part 1 proof if you
        were working in <m>\Q</m> instead of <m>\R</m>?
        Why or why not?
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod2-as-r2">
    <title>Bauldry's Proof Structure</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        In the Bridge Reading you read Bauldry's proof of Theorem 2.3
        (Archimedean Property). Compare its logical structure to the
        proof you wrote in Part 2 (Option B, <xref ref="ex-mod2-as-2b"/>) or the hint you read for
        Option B. What is the same? If there are differences, where
        did your proof and Bauldry's diverge?
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod2-as-r3">
    <title>Looking Ahead</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        In <xref ref="ch-mod4"/>, you will prove that sequences like
        <m>a_n = 1 - 1/n</m> converge to their limit using an
        <m>\varepsilon</m>-<m>N</m> argument. Based on what you
        practiced in this module, describe the connection you see
        between the <m>\varepsilon</m>-characterization of the
        supremum and the structure of an <m>\varepsilon</m>-<m>N</m>
        convergence proof. What is similar? What will be new?
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <paragraphs xml:id="par-mod2-as-rubric">
    <title>Assessment Rubric (Informational)</title>
    <p>
      The following rubric describes how submitted proofs will be
      evaluated.
    </p>
    <tabular halign="left">
      <col width="14%"/>
      <col width="29%"/>
      <col width="29%"/>
      <col width="28%"/>
      <row header="yes">
        <cell><p>Criterion</p></cell>
        <cell><p>Full credit</p></cell>
        <cell><p>Partial credit</p></cell>
        <cell><p>Minimal credit</p></cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell><p><alert>Logical correctness</alert></p></cell>
        <cell><p>All steps are valid; the argument proves exactly the stated claim</p></cell>
        <cell><p>Mostly correct with one fixable gap</p></cell>
        <cell><p>Significant logical errors or wrong claim proved</p></cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell><p><alert>Use of definitions</alert></p></cell>
        <cell><p>Both conditions of the supremum definition (or the <m>\varepsilon</m>-characterization, where required) are verified explicitly</p></cell>
        <cell><p>One condition is verified correctly; the other is sketched</p></cell>
        <cell><p>Definition not invoked or confused with a different property</p></cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell><p><alert><m>\varepsilon</m>-characterization</alert></p></cell>
        <cell><p>An explicit element of <m>S</m> in <m>(s - \varepsilon, s]</m> is exhibited with full justification</p></cell>
        <cell><p>Correct idea but the element is not explicitly verified to be in <m>S</m></p></cell>
        <cell><p>The <m>\varepsilon</m>-step is missing or circular</p></cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell><p><alert>Mathematical prose</alert></p></cell>
        <cell><p>Complete grammatical sentences; scratch work labeled and separate from proof</p></cell>
        <cell><p>Mostly prose with some missing connective sentences</p></cell>
        <cell><p>Primarily symbolic with no connecting narrative</p></cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell><p><alert>Reflection</alert></p></cell>
        <cell><p>Specific, thoughtful, and engages with the mathematical content</p></cell>
        <cell><p>General but relevant observations</p></cell>
        <cell><p>Vague or too brief to be informative</p></cell>
      </row>
    </tabular>
  </paragraphs>

</section>
